These eight are a default, not a fixture. They bind on most construction-era programmes, which is why they are watched as standard. Part of the diagnostic is establishing which actually bind on yours — domains swap one-for-one with the wider estate on that evidence, so the pack stays the same size and the same price.

They also take the shape of your asset. On a live airport, gates and induction means the pass office, the vetting queue and airside vehicle permits, and parking means contractor demand competing with revenue parking. On a rural campus it is beds and the road network. On a fab it is temporary power and tool-install movement. Deciding which eight, and what each one means on your site, is the work.

The wider estate — temporary power and utilities, foul, water and drainage, traffic management and segregation, craneage and material movement, earthworks and spoil, waste and environmental compliance, emergency access and muster, commissioning-phase logistics — is authored in full in the Execution Plan.

Against your framework

Why your existing advisors don’t already do this
Not because they can’t. Because nobody has been asked to.

A programme manager is commissioned on cost and schedule and reports on both, package by package. Capacity is neither. It is consumption, and it lives in different systems entirely — turnstile logs, car park counts, canteen tills, bus manifests, bed bookings. None of that reaches a cost report or a P6 schedule, and no line in a PM appointment says who owns the sum of it.

The gap is structural on a multi-contractor site. Each contractor plans its logistics inside its own fence, and each plan is reasonable on its own terms. They are all drawing on the same roads, the same car parks, the same induction centre. Nobody is commissioned to add them up — so the first time anyone sees the combined curve is the week it breaches.

That is the whole job, and it sits beside your framework rather than competing with it. Your programme manager keeps cost and schedule. We hold the consumption model and hand you dated decisions with the arithmetic shown.

