Common questions

Before you ask
What does Genkai mean?
限界 — Japanese for the limit: the point past which something can no longer go. Which is the whole job, really. The practice was previously called Headroom.

What data do you need?
A headcount curve and whatever capacity records exist — in any state. Lookaheads, registers, spreadsheets, PDFs, photos of whiteboards. Part of the diagnostic is establishing the baseline where records are thin.

Can I speak to someone who has worked with you?
Yes. References are available on request — including people who have run these systems at megaproject scale and directors on the contracting side. Where anyone offered has a commercial interest in DGMO, we say so before you speak to them.

Is our data safe?
Your data stays yours. Reports are project-confidential, nothing is shared across clients, and we'll sign your NDA before seeing a single number.

Which jurisdictions?
Ireland (S.I. 291/2013, as amended) live today. Great Britain runs on the CDM 2015 Schedule 2 duty with the Workplace Regulations 1992 ACOP table behind it, and Northern Ireland on its own 2016 regulations. Germany: BaustellV with ASR A4.1 and A4.2. United States: 29 CFR 1926.51, or the State Plan standard under the 22 State Plans covering private-sector construction — Arizona and California among them. Same engine, the right table per site.

How fast is the first result?
A provisional breach map lands within ten working days of your data arriving — every figure flagged at stated confidence — then hardens through verification into the final board-ready pack across the six-week scope.

